# klause working notes

Kotlin Multiplatform constraint solver.

## Pre-PR gate

Run `./gradlew check lintDocs`.

- check runs tests and some light checks.
- lintDocs runs detekt and dokka linting.
- For new/edited files add `--rerun-tasks`, detekt caches per source-set and skips them otherwise.
- detekt and dokka run on alpha analysis engines and fail intermittently. A detekt failure reporting
  **zero findings**, or a dokka `Error in translating of symbol`, with a stack frame inside the Kotlin
  analysis API rather than a file of yours, is a flake: re-run the task before investigating. Seen as
  `No receiver found in qualified expression`, `AsyncExecutionService.getService must not return null`,
  and a dokka failure on an untouched file that passes locally. A genuine violation names your file and
  the rule.

## Tests

- Test names within backticks, plain ascii, describing the behavior under test.
- Class almost always named after the file under test (`<FileUnderTest>Test`).
- One observable behavior per test; assert behavior, not implementation. Follow arrange → act → assert.
- Mirror existing tests' framework, layout, and assertion style.
- Reuse existing fixtures/helpers; don't add new ones unless asked.
- Use the parameterized form for variants of the same behavior; separate tests for semantically different ones.
- Keep comments minimal and let the test name carry the intent.
- No JVM test over 300ms.

## Bench

Read `klause-bench/README.md` before benchmarking. In short:

- Rebuild the CLI binary first with `:klause-cli:installJvmDist`, since bench runs it as a subprocess. `installDist` is
  a no-op that serves a stale binary, so suspect it when a bench run contradicts a passing test.
- `./gradlew :klause-bench:bench --args="solve backend=... suite=..."` runs solvers as subprocesses emitting MiniZinc
  output files.
- Analyze the output files separately with scripts; A/B by varying CLI params across runs.

## Style

- Prefer clear naming and types over comments. Comments explain *why* (soundness, perf, lifecycle) and never
  restate the code. Never narrate changes, avoid "now/no longer/previously", and don't cite issues. Keep `TODO`/`FIXME`.
- KDoc on public API only. Use functional notation for math (`succ(i)`, not `succ[i]`); use `[Symbol]` for links.
- Single-line conventional commits, no scope, no body/trailers:
  `feat: `, `fix: `, `chore: `, `refactor: `, `docs: `, `test: `.
- PR body: follow `.github/pull_request_template.md`, with `Closes #NN` on its own line at the top.
- Issues: follow `.github/issue_template.md`. Keep them short, a few plain sentences,
  and link code/PRs instead of pasting.

## Solver boundaries

- `ir` owns engine-neutral literals, variable descriptions, remapping, and structural keys. It does not
  depend on engines, compilation, or input formats.
- `lowering` owns generic Boolean and constraint encodings shared by schema compilation and input formats.
  It does not depend on either caller. Input formats parse and lower source syntax; they are not compiler
  dependencies.
- `propagation` owns finite-domain state, explanations, and incremental propagators. Its difference
  system follows the trail and can pin literals back into the propagation fixpoint.
- `arithmetic.difference` owns immutable difference-constraint representation, fragment extraction,
  and non-incremental graph operations. It must not depend on propagation state.
- `simplex.exact` owns reusable exact rational arithmetic and feasibility simplex primitives.
- `lp.engine` owns solving an `LpModel` and certifying the result: the simplex, its basis, and the exact
  dual/Farkas certificates. It may depend only on `util`, `simplex.exact`, and `lp.lattice` — never on
  factors, propagation, presolve, search, or the solver model. That closed list is what keeps the engine
  swappable, so widening it is the thing to refuse.
- `lp.lattice` owns exact integer matrix reduction (echelon, Hermite, unimodular transforms) and the
  bounds a triangular block implies. It is a leaf: `util` and nothing else.
- `lp` and its remaining subpackages own the model-facing half: relaxation assembly from factors,
  separators, bounding policy, open-model reasoning. Dependencies point at `lp.engine`, never back.
- `theory.difference`, `theory.lia`, and `theory.qflra` decide complete open-model fragments. They must
  not materialize finite CP domains.
- `solver.pipeline` selects an open theory once and exposes one verdict surface. Frontends render
  assignments; they do not select or invoke concrete theories.
- Dependencies point from policy to mechanism: pipeline and theories may depend on solver model types
  and arithmetic kernels; propagation may depend on immutable arithmetic representations; arithmetic
  kernels do not depend on propagation or search.
